Thanks Anna &nbsp;From: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 25 October 2025 08:22To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Fw: PDD Trial: Summary week 2 – NDC (normal driving conditions) evidence &nbsp;For insurers but ive not yet reviewed.&nbsp;&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Anna&nbsp;VargaPartner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;699From: Nichola Marshall &lt;nmarshall@leighday.co.uk&gt;Sent: Saturday, October 25, 2025 7:38:33 AMTo: Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Martyn Day &lt;mday@leighday.co.uk&gt;Subject: PDD Trial: Summary week 2 – NDC (normal driving conditions) evidence &nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Morning all &nbsp;Please see the below summary of week 2 as prepared by Milberg, should you wish to use this for your funders.&nbsp;Best &nbsp;Nichola &nbsp;&nbsp;Trial week 2 – w/c 20 October 2025Week two of the PDD Trial focused on the issue of what are conditions which are “reasonably likely to be encountered in normal vehicle operation and use” for the purposes of identifying prohibited defeat devices in the Defendants’ vehicles.The evidence on this issue includes:Expert evidence on normal driving conditions prepared by Professor Hausberger for the Mercedes Defendants and Dr Tate for the Claimants (this evidence considered data and statistics on the range of typical driving, how fast people drive, how hard they accelerate etc);&nbsp;andExpert evidence on climatology prepared by Professor Chapman for the VW Defendants and Professor Mills for the ClaimantsAdditionally, Vauxhall rely on work by Professor Pope in respect of climatology but he has not been engaged as an expert in these proceedings.&nbsp;Mr Adam Kramer KC introduced Dr Tate and his evidence and Mr Malcolm Sheehan KC conducted a cross-examination of Dr Tate for the Mercedes Defendants. Mr Sheehan KC’s cross-examination focused on a number of corrections Dr Tate had made to his report including clarifying his evidence on how long comparable vehicles spend in the operating windows of conditions which would trigger defeat devices in normal vehicle operation and use. Dr Tate had initially presented these figures as percentages of the periods of acceleration where emissions control systems would be de-rated given that emissions are mostly produced during periods of acceleration. Subsequently Dr Tate provided additional detail in respect of the periods of time during whole trips and the periods when the vehicle is moving. Mr Sheehan KC also questioned Dr Tate on whether there were errors in his calculations and attacked the comparability of the vehicles Dr Tate had with the sample vehicles in the Mercedes proceedings,&nbsp;suggesting that he had overplayed the operation window of certain defeat devices as a result.&nbsp;Mr Sheehan KC introduced Professor Hausberger and his evidence and Mr Kramer KC conducted a cross-examination of Professor Hausberger for the Claimants. Mr Kramer KC’s cross-examination focused on the significant funding which Professor Hausberger has received from the car manufacturer industry for research during his career and the objectively ridiculous conclusions he had set out in his report. For example, Professor Hausberger’s report stated that the maximum speed which could be considered normal is 101km/h (although a higher speed could be used in a journey without it being abnormal, but only if it was less than 5% of the time), this is below the speed limit on many roads. In addition to this, Professor Hausberger had stated that the maximum length of a normal journey is 46 minutes. &nbsp;Professor Hausberger was inflexible on these limits under some good cross-examination which undermined his evidence somewhat&nbsp;The climatology evidence was less contentious as&nbsp;the experts&nbsp;agreed that each other’s methods were sound and provide different statistical views on the conditions likely to be encountered. Professor Chapman&nbsp;(for the Defendants)&nbsp;provided a series of average temperatures for the UK and all the countries in the EU as well as smaller regions for different months and seasons, he also weighted these averages according to population. Professor Mills (for the Claimants) provided detail regarding the full range of temperatures experienced across the EU and the number of people who experience such temperatures and for how long in a given year. &nbsp;Both these experts provided information about altitudes which are encountered in the EU and the UK. Lady Justice Cockerill was grateful for their evidence and noted that this was unlikely to be a significant part of the judgment but that it was invaluable to have this information and the limitations of the different approaches available to her for consideration.&nbsp;We think that the Claimants’ evidence and cross-examination of the Defendants’ experts has clearly had a positive impact on the judge’s views of this element of the case. While we anticipate that the likely approach Lady Justice Cockerill will take is to set a limit on the range of conditions which would reasonably be encountered during normal vehicle operation and use, however, we do not expect that she will be inclined to adopt an excessively restrictive set of boundaries (which the Defendants have advocated for) in light of the Claimants’ case on this issue.&nbsp;We are committed to protecting your privacy when you use our services and treat all personal data in accordance with our Privacy Policy DisclaimerThe information contained in this communication from nmarshall@leighday.co.uk sent at 2025-10-24 16:20:38 is confidential and may be legally privileged. It is intended solely for use by those authorised to receive it. If you are not so authorised, you are hereby notified that any disclosure, copying, distribution or taking action in reliance upon the contents of this information is strictly prohibited and may be unlawful. We do not accept service of documents by email. Please note that we only provide our bank account details in a password protected PDF. The password will be communicated separately by telephone. We will never send changes to our bank account details by email outside of this process. If you receive such an email, do not respond to it but contact us separately via your contact at the firm. Visit the Leigh Day website: https://www.leighday.co.uk Leigh Day is a partnership authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA). The firm's SRA number is 00067679. PLEASE THINK ABOUT THE ENVIRONMENT BEFORE PRINTING THIS EMAIL &nbsp;DisclaimerThe information contained in this communication from the sender is confidential. It is intended solely for use by the recipient and others authorized to receive it. If you are not the recipient, you are hereby notified that any disclosure, copying, distribution or taking action in relation of the contents of this information is strictly prohibited and may be unlawful.This email has been scanned for viruses and malware, and may have been automatically archived by Mimecast Ltd, an innovator in Software as a Service (SaaS) for business. Providing a safer and more useful place for your human generated data. Specializing in; Security, archiving and compliance. To find out more Click Here."}

